en big gain. We don't know.\n\nAnother optimization: Use a max-heap of size k; for each point, compare with root and replace if smaller. This reduces to O(N log k) worst-case but often less because only when d < current max. For random data, about half replacements? Actually expected number of replacements is ~k/(i+1)? Not sure. But heap operations are more complex.\n\nGiven role B specifically \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\", perhaps we should focus on enabling auto-vectorization for the distance computation by using a temporary array and #pragma omp simd, or restructuring to avoid data dependency in insertion? The insertion has a loop-carried dependency (shifting), cannot vectorize. But we can use `#pragma GCC ivdep` if safe? Not.\n\nMaybe we can change algorithm to compute all distances into an array, then use nth_element or partial_sort? That would be O(N) average for selection of k smallest using quickselect; but need labels too. Could store pairs (distance, label) and use a custom selection. But that's not SIMD either.\n\nGiven the constraints, perhaps the simplest safe optimization is to replace qsort with counting frequencies directly since labels are chars (0-255). We can count occurrences in an array of 256 ints, then find max frequency with tie-breaking according to sorted order? Wait current behavior: after sorting labels ascending by char value, it scans and picks most frequent; ties broken by earliest occurrence in sorted order, i.e., smallest label character. Because when frequencies equal, only update if strictly greater, so the first encountered (smallest char) wins. So we can count frequencies for each char, then iterate chars from 0 to 255 and pick max with strict >. That avoids qsort O(k log k), but k is small anyway.\n\nBut that's not SIMD.\n\nMaybe we should use OpenMP parallelization across groups/points with reduction on top-k? Could be effective given 4 cores. But role B says \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencie