nd for d among positives? Actually if there are duplicates equal to d, they appear before larger values; condition `d < distances[x]` is false for equals, so we skip them and insert after the last duplicate. That's upper_bound (first > d). So insertion position = first index with distance > d, or k if none (but then -1 at end? Actually there will be a -1 eventually unless array full; but if full of positives all <=d, then no -1 and we don't insert because loop ends without break. The original code only inserts if it finds a position where condition true; if d is >= all existing distances and k slots are filled with positive values (no -1), then no insertion occurs. So the algorithm keeps only k smallest distinct? Actually if array full of positives and new d larger than max, it won't insert. If equal to some but not less, also won't insert because condition false for equals; so duplicates at boundary may be excluded depending on order.\n\nThus binary search should find upper_bound among positive values (ignoring -1). But we need to handle the case where there are -1s: if any -1 exists, insertion position is first -1 index. Since -1 < all positives but they appear after positives? Wait initial array has -1 at positions 0..k-1; after inserting some positives, the array becomes [positives..., -1...] because we shift right when inserting before a positive or at first -1. So -1s are always at the end. Therefore insertion position is min(upper_bound among positives, index of first -1). Since upper_bound returns first > d; if all positives <=d, it returns number_of_positives (which equals k - num_negatives), and that's exactly where first -1 starts. So we can just do binary search over the entire array treating -1 as +infinity? But -1 is less than any positive, so not good.\n\nSimpler: We know the current count of valid entries `cnt` (number of positives). Initially 0. When inserting a new d:\n- If cnt < k, we need to insert at position pos = upper_bound(distances[0..cnt-1