s exactly this: replace linear insertion with binary search + memmove, which removes loop-carried dependency (the inner x loop) and allows compiler to optimize better. Also add `restrict` or const qualifiers? But not needed.\n\nLet's also consider using a max-heap instead; but that changes order of ties maybe.\n\nI'll implement the binary search insertion in knn.c, keeping everything else same. This is safe and likely gives some speedup if k is large. Also we can add `#pragma GCC ivdep` on distance loop? Not needed.\n\nBut to satisfy role B \"legal SIMD/vectorization\", perhaps we should also restructure the point loops to be vectorizable by computing distances into a temporary array per group, then process insertion sequentially. However that adds memory and may not help if compiler already auto-vectorizes simple arithmetic.\n\nLet's check: The distance computation `euclidean_distance_no_sqrt` is called in inner loop; it does two subtractions, multiplications, addition. Compiler can vectorize this loop over j if it knows alignment and no aliasing? But because we call a function (even inline), the compiler may not vectorize due to potential side effects? The function is defined in header as non-static, but likely inlined at -O3. Still, the loop has an external call? Actually after inlining, it's just arithmetic. However the loop also contains the insertion code which modifies arrays; that creates a dependency between iterations because distances array changes and labels change, so compiler cannot vectorize the whole loop body. But if we split into two loops: first compute all d for points in group into a temporary local array `float ds[g.length]` (VLA or malloc), then second loop over j does insertion using ds[j]. The first loop is pure and can be vectorized with SIMD. This might improve performance significantly if g.length large.\n\nWe could do that: For each group, allocate an array of floats of size length (or use VLA? C99 supports VLA but maybe not safe; we can