nces into ds[0..g.length-1] with SIMD pragma.\n- Then insertion loop using binary search.\n\nAlso we can use `restrict` on pointers to help compiler? Not necessary.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe need to preserve original behavior exactly. Let's test mentally:\n\nOriginal insertion:\n```\nfor (x = 0; x < k; x++) {\n    if (d < distances[x] || distances[x] == -1) {\n        for (y = k - 1; y > x; y--) {\n            distances[y] = distances[y - 1];\n            labels[y] = labels[y - 1];\n        }\n        distances[x] = d;\n        labels[x] = g.label;\n        break;\n    }\n}\n```\nThis shifts all elements from x to k-2 right by one (since loop y from k-1 down to x+1, copying y-1 to y). So it drops the last element if array full. If there are -1s at end, they get shifted too.\n\nOur binary search insertion:\nWe maintain `cnt` = number of valid entries? But we don't need cnt; we can just use k and treat -1 as sentinel. However for binary search, we need to know the range of positive values. We could keep a variable `valid_count` that tracks how many distances have been inserted (i.e., not -1). Initially 0. After each insertion, if valid_count < k, increment; else stays k.\n\nBut note: The original algorithm may insert into positions beyond current valid count? For example, first point d=5, array all -1. Loop x=0 sees -1 -> insert at 0, shift none. Now distances[0]=5, rest -1. valid_count becomes 1. Second point d=3: loop x=0: 3<5 true -> insert at 0, shift distances[0] to [1], labels too; now distances[0]=3, [1]=5, rest -1. valid_count becomes 2 (since we shifted a positive into position 1). So valid_count increments by 1 each insertion until k.\n\nSo we can maintain `int cnt = 0;` representing number of non-negative entries? But distances are floats; initial -1 is negative. All real distances >=0, so any value >=0 is valid. We'll use `cnt`.\n\nInsertion algorithm:\n```\nif (d < distances[cnt-1] || cnt == 0) { // if there's room or d smaller than max\n    // find pos = first index i in [0,cnt) such that distances[i] > d, else cnt\n    int lo=0, hi=cnt; // binary search for upper_bound\n    while (lo < hi) {\n        int mid = (lo+hi)/2;\n        if (distances[mid] <= d) lo = mid+1; else hi = mid;\n    }\n    int pos = lo; // first > d\n    // shift elements from pos to cnt-1 right by one, but only up to k-1\n    if (cnt < k) {\n        for (int y=cnt; y>pos; --y) { distances[y]=distances[y-1]; labels[y]=labels[y-1]; }\n        distances[pos]=d; labels[pos]=g.label;\n        cnt++;\n    } else { // full, shift and drop last\n        if (pos < k) {\n            for (int y=k-1; y>pos; --y) { distances[y]=distances[y-1]; labels[y]=labels[y-1]; }\n            distances[pos]=d; labels[pos]=g.label;\n        } // else d >= all, no insert\n    }\n}\n```\nBut we need to handle the case where cnt==0: then pos=0 and shift none.\n\nAlso condition for insertion when full: original inserts if there exists x with d < distances[x] (strict). That is equivalent to d < max distance. If d equals max but there are duplicates, no insert because strict <. Our binary search finds first > d; if all <=d, pos=cnt=k, and we don't insert. Good.\n\nBut what about when cnt<k? Original always inserts because eventually it will find a -1 at position cnt (since array has -1s after valid entries). So condition `cnt < k` ensures insertion regardless of d relative to existing positives; even if d is larger than all, pos=cnt and we insert at end. That matches original: loop scans x from 0..k-1; it will skip all positives (since d not less), then hit -1 at index cnt and insert there. So yes.\n\nSo our logic works.\n\nWe can use `memmove` for shifting instead of manual loops to potentially leverage vectorized copy:\n```\nif (cnt < k) {\n    memmove(&distances[pos+1], &distances[pos], (cnt-pos)*sizeof(float));\n    memmove(&labels[pos+1], &labels[pos], (cnt-pos)*sizeof(char));\n} else if (pos < k) {\n    memmove(&distances[pos+1], &distances[pos], (k-pos-1)*sizeof(